<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Background</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Diffusion Models</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sohl-Dickstein et al., 2015</a>)</cite> constitute a state-of-the-art family of generative models that can be formulated via stochastic differential equations, diffusion processes, and score-based modeling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2021</a>)</cite>. They have achieved strong results across images, video, audio, time series, and more <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Berman et al. (2025b)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gonen et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Naiman et al. (2024a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kong et al. (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Berman et al. (2024a)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al. (2022)</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Berman et al. (2025a)</a></cite>. We will use diffusion models and score-based models interchangeably. These models include two processes: the forward process and the reverse process. The forward process (often not learnable) is an iterative procedure that corrupts the data by progressively adding noise to it. Specifically, the change to the state <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> can be formally described by</p>
<table id="A1.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}_{t}=\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t)\mathrm{d}t+{g}(t)\mathrm{d}\mathbf{w}\ ," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝐰</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.500em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}_{t}=\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t)\mathrm{d}t+{g}(t)\mathrm{d}\mathbf{w}\ ,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐰</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}</annotation></semantics></math> is the standard Wiener process, <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{f}(\cdot,t)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{f}(\cdot,t)</annotation></semantics></math> is a vector-valued function called the drift coefficient, and <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{g}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{g}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> is a scalar function known as the diffusion coefficient. From a probabilistic viewpoint, Eq. <a href="#A1.E7" title="Equation 7 ‣ A.1 Diffusion Models ‣ Appendix A Background ‣ DiffSDA: Unsupervised Diffusion Sequential Disentanglement Across Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> is associated with modeling the transition from the given data distribution, <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{0}\sim p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{0}\sim p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, to <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the probability density of <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,T]</annotation></semantics></math>. Typically, the prior distribution <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> is a simple Gaussian distribution with fixed mean and variance that contains no information of <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. The reverse process, which is learnable, de-noises the data iteratively. The reverse of a diffusion process is also a diffusion process, depending on the score function <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{x})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{x})</annotation></semantics></math> and operating in reverse time <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anderson, 1982</a>)</cite>. In our approach, we utilize the conditioned reverse process</p>
<table id="A1.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}_{t}=[\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t)-g(t)^{2}\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}\mid\mathbf{u})]\mathrm{d}\bar{t}+g(t)\mathrm{d}\bar{\mathbf{w}}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝐟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{d}\mathbf{x}_{t}=[\mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}_{t},t)-g(t)^{2}\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}\mid\mathbf{u})]\mathrm{d}\bar{t}+g(t)\mathrm{d}\bar{\mathbf{w}},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{w}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐰</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{w}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a standard Wiener process as time progresses backward from <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{d}\bar{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>t</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{d}\bar{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is an negative timestep, and <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}</annotation></semantics></math> is a condition variable. Diffusion models are generative by sampling from <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and use <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}\mid\mathbf{u})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>𝐱</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>𝐮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\nabla_{\mathbf{x}}\log p_{t}(\mathbf{x}_{t}\mid\mathbf{u})</annotation></semantics></math> to iteratively solve Eq. <a href="#A1.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ A.1 Diffusion Models ‣ Appendix A Background ‣ DiffSDA: Unsupervised Diffusion Sequential Disentanglement Across Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> until samples from <math id="A1.SS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> are recovered.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Diffusion Autoencoders</h3>

<div id="A1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Although diffusion models are powerful generative tools, they are not inherently designed to learn meaningful representations of the data. To address this limitation, several works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Preechakul et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2023</a>)</cite> have adapted diffusion models into autoencoders, resulting in diffusion autoencoders (DiffAEs). These models have demonstrated the ability to learn semantic representations of the data, allowing certain modifications of the resulting samples by altering their latent vectors. To this end, DiffAEs introduce a semantic encoder, taking a data sample <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and returning its semantic latent encoding <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\text{sem}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mtext>sem</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\text{sem}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then, the latter vector conditions the reverse process, enhancing the model’s ability to reconstruct and manipulate data samples. In practice, the denoiser is also conditioned on a feature map <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>h</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h</annotation></semantics></math> and the time <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, combined using an adaptive group normalization (AdaGN) layer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dhariwal &amp; Nichol, 2021</a>)</cite>. The AdaGN block is defined as</p>
<table id="A1.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A1.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{AdaGN}(h,t,z_{\text{sem}})=z_{s}\left(t_{s}\,\text{GroupNorm}(h)+t_{b}\right)\ ," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>AdaGN</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mtext>sem</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>GroupNorm</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>b</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.500em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{AdaGN}(h,t,z_{\text{sem}})=z_{s}\left(t_{s}\,\text{GroupNorm}(h)+t_{b}\right)\ ,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is the output of a linear layer applied to <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{\text{sem}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mtext>sem</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{\text{sem}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{b}</annotation></semantics></math> are the outputs of a multi-layer perceptron (MLP) applied to the time <math id="A1.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, and multiplications are done element-wise.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>DiffSDA Modeling</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Unsupervised Sequential Disentanglement</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unsupervised sequential disentanglement is a challenging problem in representation learning, aiming to decompose a given dataset to its static (time-independent) and dynamic (time-dependent) factors of variation. Let <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{D}=\{\textbf{{x}}_{j}^{1:V}\}_{j=1}^{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">x</mtext><mi>j</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}=\{\textbf{{x}}_{j}^{1:V}\}_{j=1}^{N}</annotation></semantics></math> be a dataset with <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> sequences <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textbf{{x}}_{j}^{1:V}:=\{\textbf{{x}}_{j}^{1},\dots,\textbf{{x}}_{j}^{V}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">x</mtext><mi>j</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">x</mtext><mi>j</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">x</mtext><mi>j</mi><mi>V</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{{x}}_{j}^{1:V}:=\{\textbf{{x}}_{j}^{1},\dots,\textbf{{x}}_{j}^{V}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textbf{{x}}_{j}^{\tau}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">x</mtext><mi>j</mi><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{{x}}_{j}^{\tau}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. We omit the subscript <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math> for brevity, unless noted otherwise. The goal of sequential disentanglement is to extract an alternative representation of <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textbf{{x}}^{1:V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">x</mtext><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{{x}}^{1:V}</annotation></semantics></math> via a single static factor <span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">s</span> and multiple dynamic factors <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textbf{{d}}^{1:V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">d</mtext><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{{d}}^{1:V}</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that <span id="A2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_bold">s</span> is shared across the sequence.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We can formalize the sequential disentanglement problem as a <em id="A2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">generative task</em>, where every sequence <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textbf{{x}}^{1:V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">x</mtext><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{{x}}^{1:V}</annotation></semantics></math> from the data space <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is conditioned on some <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textbf{{z}}^{1:V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">z</mtext><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{{z}}^{1:V}</annotation></semantics></math> from a latent space <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. We aim to maximize the probability of each sequence under the entire generative process</p>
<table id="A2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A2.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(\textbf{{x}}^{1:V})=\int_{\mathcal{Z}}p(\textbf{{x}}^{1:V}\mid\textbf{{z}}^{1:V})p(\textbf{{z}}^{1:V})\dd\textbf{{z}}^{1:V}\ ," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">x</mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">x</mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">z</mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">z</mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0.167em">d</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">z</mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\textbf{{x}}^{1:V})=\int_{\mathcal{Z}}p(\textbf{{x}}^{1:V}\mid\textbf{{z}}^{1:V})p(\textbf{{z}}^{1:V})\dd\textbf{{z}}^{1:V}\ ,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textbf{{z}}^{1:V}:=(\textbf{{s}},\textbf{{d}}^{1:V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">z</mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">s</mtext><mo>,</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">d</mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{{z}}^{1:V}:=(\textbf{{s}},\textbf{{d}}^{1:V})</annotation></semantics></math>. One of the main challenges with directly maximizing Eq. (<a href="#A2.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ B.1 Unsupervised Sequential Disentanglement ‣ Appendix B DiffSDA Modeling ‣ DiffSDA: Unsupervised Diffusion Sequential Disentanglement Across Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) is that the latent space <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> is too large to practically integrate over. Instead, a separate distribution, denoted here as <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q(\textbf{{z}}^{1:V}\mid\textbf{{x}}^{1:V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">z</mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">x</mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\textbf{{z}}^{1:V}\mid\textbf{{x}}^{1:V})</annotation></semantics></math>, is used to narrow search to be only over <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\textbf{{z}}^{1:V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">z</mtext><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textbf{{z}}^{1:V}</annotation></semantics></math> associated with sequences from the dataset <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. Importantly, the distributions <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(\textbf{{x}}^{1:V}\mid\textbf{{z}}^{1:V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">x</mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">z</mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\textbf{{x}}^{1:V}\mid\textbf{{z}}^{1:V})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q(\textbf{{z}}^{1:V}\mid\textbf{{x}}^{1:V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">z</mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">x</mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\textbf{{z}}^{1:V}\mid\textbf{{x}}^{1:V})</annotation></semantics></math> take the form of a decoder and an encoder in practice, suggesting the development of <em id="A2.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">autoencoder</em> sequential disentanglement models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yingzhen &amp; Mandt, 2018</a>)</cite>. The above <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(\textbf{{x}}^{1:V}\mid\textbf{{z}}^{1:V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">x</mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">z</mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\textbf{{x}}^{1:V}\mid\textbf{{z}}^{1:V})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m13" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q(\textbf{{z}}^{1:V}\mid\textbf{{x}}^{1:V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">z</mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">x</mtext><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\textbf{{z}}^{1:V}\mid\textbf{{x}}^{1:V})</annotation></semantics></math> are denoted by <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{T0}(\textbf{{x}}_{0}^{\tau}\mid\textbf{{x}}_{T}^{\tau},\textbf{{s}}_{0},\textbf{{d}}_{0}^{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">x</mtext><mn>0</mn><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msubsup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">x</mtext><mi>T</mi><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">s</mtext><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">d</mtext><mn>0</mn><mi>τ</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{T0}(\textbf{{x}}_{0}^{\tau}\mid\textbf{{x}}_{T}^{\tau},\textbf{{s}}_{0},\textbf{{d}}_{0}^{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p(\textbf{{x}}_{t}^{1:V},\textbf{{s}}_{0},\textbf{{d}}_{0}^{1:V}\mid\textbf{{x}}_{0}^{1:V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">x</mtext><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">s</mtext><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">d</mtext><mn>0</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msubsup><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">x</mtext><mn>0</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p(\textbf{{x}}_{t}^{1:V},\textbf{{s}}_{0},\textbf{{d}}_{0}^{1:V}\mid\textbf{{x}}_{0}^{1:V})</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively, in Eq. <a href="#S3.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 3.1 Probabilistic Modeling ‣ 3 Method ‣ DiffSDA: Unsupervised Diffusion Sequential Disentanglement Across Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and Eq. <a href="#S3.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 3.1 Probabilistic Modeling ‣ 3 Method ‣ DiffSDA: Unsupervised Diffusion Sequential Disentanglement Across Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>High-Resolution Disentangled Sequential Diffusion Autoencoder</h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to transitioning to real-world data, our goal is to manage high-resolution data for unsupervised sequential disentanglement, for the first time. Drawing inspiration from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rombach et al. (2022)</a></cite>, we incorporate perceptual image compression, which combines an autoencoder with a perceptual loss <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2018</a>)</cite> and a patch-based adversarial objective <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Dosovitskiy &amp; Brox, 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Esser et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Isola et al., 2017</a>)</cite>. Specifically, we explore two main variants of the autoencoder. The first variant applies a small Kullback–Leibler penalty to encourage the learned latent space to approximate a standard normal distribution, similar to a VAE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma, 2013</a>; <a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rezende et al., 2014</a>)</cite>. The second variant integrates a vector quantization layer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Van Den Oord et al., 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Razavi et al., 2019</a>)</cite> within the decoder. Empirically, we find that the VQ-VAE-based model performs better when combined with our method. Given a pre-trained encoder <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> and decoder <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can extract <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{0}^{\tau}=\mathcal{E}(\mathrm{x}_{0}^{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{0}^{\tau}=\mathcal{E}(\mathrm{x}_{0}^{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math>, which represents a low-dimensional latent space where high-frequency, imperceptible details are abstracted away. Finally, <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{x}_{0}^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{x}_{0}^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> can be reconstructed from the latent <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{0}^{\tau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{0}^{\tau}</annotation></semantics></math> by applying the decoder <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{x}_{0}^{\tau}=\mathcal{D}(\mathbf{x}_{0}^{\tau})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐱</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>τ</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{x}_{0}^{\tau}=\mathcal{D}(\mathbf{x}_{0}^{\tau})</annotation></semantics></math>. The EDM formulation in Eq. <a href="#S3.E4" title="Equation 4 ‣ Decoder. ‣ 3.2 Diffusion Sequential Disentanglement Autoencoder ‣ 3 Method ‣ DiffSDA: Unsupervised Diffusion Sequential Disentanglement Across Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> makes relatively strong assumptions about the mean and standard deviation of the training data. To meet these assumptions, we opt to normalize the training data globally rather than adjusting the value of <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which could significantly affect other hyperparameters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Therefore, we keep <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\text{data}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mtext>data</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\text{data}}</annotation></semantics></math> at its default value of 0.5 and ensure that the latents have a zero mean during dataset preprocessing. When generating sequence elements, we reverse this normalization before applying <math id="A2.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Prior Modeling</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We model the prior static and dynamic distribution with <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{T0}(\bm{s}_{0},\bm{d}_{0}^{1:V}\mid\bm{s}_{T},\bm{d}_{T}^{1:V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒅</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒅</mi><mi>T</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{T0}(\bm{s}_{0},\bm{d}_{0}^{1:V}\mid\bm{s}_{T},\bm{d}_{T}^{1:V})</annotation></semantics></math>. To sample static and dynamic factors, we train a separate latent DDIM model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. Then, we can extract the factors by sampling noise, and reversing the trained model. Specifically, we learn <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p_{\Delta t}(\mathbf{z}_{t-1}^{1:V}\mid\mathbf{z}_{t}^{1:V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\Delta t}(\mathbf{z}_{t-1}^{1:V}\mid\mathbf{z}_{t}^{1:V})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{0}=(\bm{s}_{0},\bm{d}_{0}^{1:V})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝒔</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝒅</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{0}=(\bm{s}_{0},\bm{d}_{0}^{1:V})</annotation></semantics></math> are the outputs of our sequential semantic encoder. The training is done by simply optimizing the <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{latent}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>latent</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{latent}}</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to DDIM’s output <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{\phi}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{\phi}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E11.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{latent}}=\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{z}^{1:V},\varepsilon_{t}}\left[\lVert\varepsilon_{\phi}(\mathbf{z}_{t}^{1:V},t)-\varepsilon_{t}\rVert\right]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>latent</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover></mstyle><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>ε</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{latent}}=\sum_{t=1}^{T}\mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{z}^{1:V},\varepsilon_{t}}\left[\lVert\varepsilon_{\phi}(\mathbf{z}_{t}^{1:V},t)-\varepsilon_{t}\rVert\right]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varepsilon_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\,V+s}\sim\mathcal{N}\big(\mathbf{0},~\mathbf{I}\big)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ε</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mn>𝟎</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐈</mi><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_{t}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\,V+s}\sim\mathcal{N}\big(\mathbf{0},~\mathbf{I}\big)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>V</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V</annotation></semantics></math> is the sequence length, <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s,d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s,d</annotation></semantics></math> are the static and dynamic factors dimensions respectively. Additionally, <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t}^{1:V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐳</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t}^{1:V}</annotation></semantics></math> is the noise version of <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> as described in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al. (2020)</a></cite>. For designing the architecture of our latent model, we follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Preechakul et al. (2022)</a></cite> and it is based on <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math> MLP layers. Our network architecture and hyperparamters are provided in Tab. <a href="#A3.T8" title="Table 8 ‣ Appendix C Hyper-parameters ‣ DiffSDA: Unsupervised Diffusion Sequential Disentanglement Across Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.4 </span>Reverse processes</h3>

<div id="A2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The detailed reverse sampling algorithm is provided in Alg. <a href="#A2.SS4" title="B.4 Reverse processes ‣ Appendix B DiffSDA Modeling ‣ DiffSDA: Unsupervised Diffusion Sequential Disentanglement Across Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>. We follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karras et al. (2022)</a></cite> sampling techniques, however, each step in our reverse process is conditioned on the latent static and dynamic factors extracted by our sequential semantic encoder. As in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Preechakul et al. (2022)</a></cite>, we observe that auto-encoding is improved significantly when using the stochastic encoding technique. Since we have a different reverse process, we provide the algorithm for stochastic encoding for our modeling in Alg. <a href="#A2.SS4" title="B.4 Reverse processes ‣ Appendix B DiffSDA Modeling ‣ DiffSDA: Unsupervised Diffusion Sequential Disentanglement Across Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>. Finally, when performing conditional swapping, we observe that performing stochastic encoding on the sample from which we borrow the dynamics and using it as an input to Alg. <a href="#A2.SS4" title="B.4 Reverse processes ‣ Appendix B DiffSDA Modeling ‣ DiffSDA: Unsupervised Diffusion Sequential Disentanglement Across Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>, improves the results empirically. That is, given two sample videos <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{x}}\sim p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{x}}\sim p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, to create a new sample <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>, conditioned on the static factor of <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bf{x}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bf{x}</annotation></semantics></math> and dynamic features of <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we use the stochastic encoding of <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{x}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐱</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{x}}</annotation></semantics></math> in Alg. <a href="#A2.SS4" title="B.4 Reverse processes ‣ Appendix B DiffSDA Modeling ‣ DiffSDA: Unsupervised Diffusion Sequential Disentanglement Across Modalities" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<br class="ltx_break ltx_break">
<div id="alg1.1" class="ltx_listing ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
              
              
            <span id="alg1.l1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">procedure</span> <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">ConditionedStochasticSampler</span>(<math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{\theta},~t_{i\in\{0,\dots,N\}},~\gamma_{i\in\{0,\dots,N-1\}},~\mathbf{z}_{0}^{1:V},\mathbf{x}_{0}^{1:V},~S_{\text{noise}}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mi>t</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mi>γ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mi>𝐳</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mi>S</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>noise</mtext></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{\theta},~t_{i\in\{0,\dots,N\}},~\gamma_{i\in\{0,\dots,N-1\}},~\mathbf{z}_{0}^{1:V},\mathbf{x}_{0}^{1:V},~S_{\text{noise}}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>)

</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
              
              
              <span id="alg1.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{0}^{1:V}\neq\text{None}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>0</mn></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup><mo>≠</mo><mtext>None</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{0}^{1:V}\neq\text{None}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
              
              
               <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{N}^{1:V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝐱</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>N</mi></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{N}^{1:V}</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2 output</span>

